  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 350 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nAn upper level low is currently digging into the southwestern\nU.S. early this morning. The low will continue to slowly dig to\nthe southwest today into Wednesday then will start to lift to\nthe northeast into the central Plains Wednesday night into\nThursday. The Low will then slow and open into a trough then \nmove east through the area on Friday. Moisture will start to \nadvect north into the area ahead of the system and the remnants \nof Hurricane Polo will also merge into the upper level low also \nincreasing moisture levels with the system. \n\nAs the upper level low start to lift northeast Wednesday night,\nbands of showers and thunderstorms will develop ahead of the\ntrough across portions of Texas and Oklahoma and lift \nnortheast, moving into the area late Wednesday night into \nThursday morning. Some weak elevated instability will develop \nalong and west of I-49 late Wednesday evening into Wednesday \nnight so some scattered showers and storms will be possible \novernight Wednesday ahead of these bands of showers and storms.\nThe storm motion will be to the northeast and the lift will \nremain across the western portions of the area through a good \nportions of the day on Thursday as the low remains west of the \narea. Therefore, bands of showers and storms will likely develop\nand occur at times from Wednesday night and much of Thursday \nmainly west of Highway 65. Later Thursday into Thursday night \nthe now trough will start to move east and the showers and \nstorms will start to move east across the rest of the area \ninto Friday morning. The rain will not remain over the eastern \nportions of the area as long as they will west of Highway 65 \nwhere the heaviest rainfall is expected. With the increase \nmoisture precipitable water values will increase to around 2\", \nwhich is high for this time of year. Therefore, the showers and \nstorms will be efficient rain producers and where training can \noccur heavy rainfall will occur. Models continue to indicate \nthe potential for bands of 2 to 3\" of rainfall occurring across\nportions of the area mainly west of Highway 65, with locally \nheavier amounts possible where training of storms can occur. \nAmounts east of Highway 65 are generally expected to be up to 1 \nto 2\". \n\nThe slow movement of the system on Thursday will likely keep \nthe eastern portions of the area dry until later in the day.  \nAcross the western portions of the area, rain will likely occur\nat times through much of the day. As a result highs are \nexpected to warm into the middle to upper 80s across the eastern\nOzarks on Thursday again well above normal for this time of year.\nTemperatures across the west within the rain, will likely not \nwarm much with highs in the 70s occurring. Southerly winds will\noccur again on Wednesday and some gusts up to around 30mph will\nbe possible at times. As the system move east Thursday there \ncould be the potential for some strong storms mainly along and \nsouth of I-44 Thursday afternoon and evening with gusty winds \nthe main risk. \n\nA cold front will move south through the area on Friday, the\nupper level trough will also be moving east across the area\nleading to some scattered showers and a few storms occurring\nalong the front Friday into Friday night. There are still some\ntiming differences on how fast the front pushes south of the\narea, from as early as Friday night to as late as during the \nday on Saturday. Overall, the models are trending drier for \nthis weekend after the front pushes south of the area Friday \nnight into Saturday. Cooler conditions will occur behind the \nfront with highs in the upper 60s into the 70s this weekend into\nearly next week.",
